/* Cascade fusion for the reference profile: multiply two spectra bin by bin.
 *
 * Setup only, not part of the steady state.  Convolving a chain of FIR
 * responses into one composite is a product in the frequency domain, so the
 * host transforms each response in turn and runs this once per response
 * after the first, accumulating in place.  Working in double-double is the
 * point: the composite is what every later block is convolved with, so its
 * error is paid on every sample.
 *
 * Bindings, in the order the fusion path in fir_vulkan.c writes them.  Set 0
 * is implicit here, as it is the only set:
 *   0 input_spectrum        -- in: the response just transformed.
 *   1 accumulated_spectrum  -- in/out: the composite so far, seeded with the
 *                              first response and multiplied in place.
 *
 * Push constant: spectrum_bins, the bin count of both buffers, fft_size / 2
 * + 1 for the transform size the host chose to hold the composite without
 * circular aliasing.  There is no channel axis: fusion works on one response
 * at a time.
 *
 * One invocation per bin, dispatched as spectrum_bins rounded up to whole
 * workgroups of 256, hence the bounds test.  Each invocation touches a single
 * distinct element of the accumulator, so reading and writing it needs no
 * synchronisation within the dispatch; the host places a barrier between
 * successive responses.
 *
